

SCIENTIFIC WHITE SHEET v1.0

Programmable Dual‑Material Growth Engine for Autonomous Subterranean Structures

Author: C. Matthew BlackwellSystem Class: Dual‑Material Bio‑Mechanical Growth ArchitectureDomains: Morphogenesis • Swarm Intelligence • Geotechnical Adaptation • 4D Fabrication

1. System Identity

The system is a programmable growth engine composed of two interacting material fields:

Rigid Field (Bone Analog) — high‑stiffness, load‑bearing lattice

Flexible Field (Tendon/Cartilage Analog) — viscoelastic damping and adaptive wrapping

The organism grows, adapts, and self‑heals in response to:

geostatic pressure

seismic excitation

soil‑interface gradients

internal damage accumulation

swarm‑level pathfinding logic

The architecture behaves as a distributed mechanical intelligence.

2. Governing Fields

2.1 Rigid Density Field ( \rho_r )

Controls structural stiffness, compressive strength, and axial load routing.

2.2 Flexible Density Field ( \rho_f )

Controls damping, shear absorption, and tendon‑like wrapping.

2.3 Damage Field ( D )

Accumulates under stress; drives erosion and healing cycles.

2.4 Signal Potential Field ( \phi )

Routes microfluidic agents toward high‑stress or damaged regions.

2.5 Vibration Amplitude Field ( v )

Encodes local cyclic stress; drives tendon reinforcement.

2.6 Soil‑Interface Gradient ( G_s )

Defines soft–hard transitions, liquefaction pockets, and impedance mismatches.

3. Evolution Equations

3.1 Rigid Deposition

[ \frac{\partial \rho_r}{\partial t} = k_r , \sigma_c^+ - \lambda_r D ] Rigid material grows where compressive stress ( \sigma_c ) is positive.

3.2 Flexible Deposition

[ \frac{\partial \rho_f}{\partial t} = k_f , v - \lambda_f D ] Flexible material grows where vibration amplitude is high.

3.3 Damage Accumulation

[ \frac{\partial D}{\partial t} = \alpha \sigma_{eq} - \beta \phi ]

3.4 Healing / Microfluidic Routing

[ \frac{\partial \phi}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot (D \nabla \phi) - \gamma \phi ]

3.5 Soil‑Interface Coupling

[ \sigma_{eff} = \sigma \cdot (1 + G_s) ]

3.6 Swarm Pathfinding Logic

Growth tips follow: [ \mathbf{v}_{tip} = -\nabla H ] where[ H = w_1 G_s + w_2 v + w_3 D ]

